AB  - University–industry collaboration is widely promoted as a pathway to green innovation, yet many studies treat patents, publications, or project outputs as substitutes for measured ecological change. Our systematic literature review synthesizes how innovative university–industry R&D collaborations relate to positive ecological outcomes and identifies the reported mechanisms and enabling conditions that translate collaboration into environmental outcomes. Using a PRISMA-guided approach across Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar (2000 to 2025), the review screened 1831 records and included 62 studies. The study provides an evidence map that integrates (i) how the literature operationalizes university-industry collaboration, (ii) the dominant knowledge and technology pathways, and (iii) synthesizes reported mechanisms associated with innovation outputs and ecological outcome evidence. To strengthen cross-study comparability, we introduced Porter’s (2010) tiered outcome logic. The framework distinguishes Tier 1 direct ecological performance indicators, Tier 2 implementation-oriented ecological evidence, Tier 3a validated green innovation outputs, and Tier 3b environmental-oriented collaboration effectiveness evidence. The synthesis revealed a persistent translation gap: recent research increasingly evidences environmental relevance through innovation proxies and collaboration-effectiveness framings rather than consistently measured ecological performance.
ER  - 

