AB  - Industrial parks have become important sites for university–industry collaboration (UIC), yet existing research offers limited explanation of why particular UIC models emerge, evolve, and work unevenly across institutional contexts. This study develops a hierarchical multi-level framework in which field embeddedness shapes institutional linkages and Actor Endowments, which in turn condition UIC model selection. Using survey data from 412 respondents across 16 Chinese industrial parks and comparative case evidence from four focal parks, supplemented by RTP as an international reference, we identify four analytically ordered UIC models: talent exchange, technology transfer, value co-creation, and ecosystem symbiosis. The SEM results show that Actor Endowments exert the strongest direct association with UIC model selection (β = 0.482, p < 0.001), whereas field embeddedness operates mainly through indirect pathways via institutional linkages and Actor Endowments. These mechanisms vary across park types and regional development levels. The study contributes to UIC research by explaining model differentiation and progressive upgrading through a contextualized multi-level framework, offering implications for policy, park management, and collaborative practice.
ER  - 

